<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sulmona</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sulmo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sulmona, the ancient Sulmo, is a town in the Peligna valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, originally one of the main towns of the Paeligni. It became a Roman municipium after the Social War and is best known as the birthplace of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a> in 43 BCE. Its medieval centre includes the aqueduct of 1256 and the Annunziata complex.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sulmona.jpg" alt="Sulmona" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lorenzo Testa · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_di_Sulmona.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
